From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:29:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] gallium: reclaim a driver's memory and retry a refused
 request

A driver refuses an allocation when the process's budget is full, and a
budget that is full often still holds memory for work that has finished:
buffers a finished batch references, storage the driver caches for
reuse. A request dropped for it is lost without a word.

u_reclaim is how a caller that can run its request again does so, once.
u_reclaim_begin() flushes the caller's context, waits for the work it
submitted, trims the context, so that completed work releases what it
holds, waits for the trim, and releases what the screen caches.
u_reclaim_settle() then waits for the room to show in the host's
budget, which a release reaches up to a quarter of a second late: 300
ms at most, 20 ms when the reclaim freed nothing worth waiting for,
which a screen reports through the new optional
pipe_screen::get_freed_memory. The caller runs its request once more and
tells u_reclaim_end(). The retry never runs inside the helper.

The waiting the reclaim adds after the flush has returned is bounded by
a deadline of 2 s that both waits for the GPU share. They poll the
fence without blocking, so that a driver that waits for the submission
and then for the GPU cannot give each the whole time, and a fence that
is ready only after the deadline does not count; the scheduler and the
driver's callbacks can run past it by what they take. The flush itself,
and a call that synchronizes the threaded context first, wait as they
always do. Work that does not finish in time ends the reclaim and the
request stays refused.

What a reclaim remembers is in the screen and the context, which carry
it zeroed like the rest of them: pipe_screen::reclaim holds the one
reclaim that runs on the screen, from its begin to its end, so that
contexts that meet a refusal together do not all wait, and the ten
seconds the screen's reclaims are ruled out for after work that did not
finish, since a GPU that is not finishing is not worth waiting for again
soon; an end that is not that of the reclaim holding the claim does
nothing. pipe_context::reclaim_futile_until_ns rules out another
reclaim of a context for 250 ms after one that did not help, since a
budget that is really full gains nothing from it and a caller that keeps
meeting the refusal would otherwise finish its context for every
request. One context's cooldown does not rule out another's, and
neither outlives its object or is shared with a new one at its address.

u_texture_subdata_try() is u_default_texture_subdata() that says whether
it wrote.

The gallium-reclaim test runs u_reclaim against contexts and screens
that answer as it says, with its times shortened: the steps of a
reclaim, the wait for the room, the cooldowns, a reclaim whose flush is
slow on another thread that keeps the screen until it ends while a late
end of an earlier one changes nothing, many contexts and screens, new
objects at old addresses, a fence that never signals, one submitted late
with the GPU still pending, one ready just after the deadline, two waits
that share the time, and a slow flush.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
